WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.080
Film Courage: Dave, I understand that you
dropped out of film school?

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.080
Dave McCary: I did.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.450
Film Courage: [You were there for] 2 years?

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.450
Dave McCary: Yes.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.240
Film Courage: I’m curious what classes you
would have preferred them to teach you?

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.750
Dave McCary: Uggh.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.680
[Sighs] Film Courage: [Laughs] Sorry to bring
this up!

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.930
Dave McCary: No…it’s fine.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.300
I would just never do it.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.630
It’s not like I wanted to learn in that
way.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:31.519
I just wanted to make videos and learn by
myself.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:39.120
Knowing now and around that time that Youtube
was just starting to become a thing and you

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.631
could find a tutorial on anything if you were
troubleshooting something or didn’t know

00:00:43.631 --> 00:00:52.559
how to work a specific editing function or
lighting issue.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:58.100
I just wanted to teach myself when I hit a
roadblock.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.930
I just knew how easy it was to find the answer
on the Internet.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:07.229
And also not go into debt.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:16.229
I was very fortunate to have my best friend
who was at USC and making all of these wonderful

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:23.320
friends in film and television and music and
I ended up just kind of mooching off of his

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.320
experience really.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:31.840
I dropped out of school and I would hang out
with these dudes and start making videos with

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.820
them because they were so talented and funny.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:40.340
It was just natural, like I don’t need to
spend money on school when I can just do it

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.149
myself and have pretty much everything I need
with a camera and some funny friends.

00:01:46.149 --> 00:01:48.409
Film Courage: This was for the Good Neighbor
Youtube Channel?

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:49.929
Dave McCary: Yes, that’s Good Neighbor.

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:55.270
We started Good Neighbor in like 2006?

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.559
Right around the time that Kyle graduated
college.

00:01:57.559 --> 00:02:01.030
And I was doing freelance editing.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:07.270
You learn so much more from just having a
job or PA-ing or assistant editing.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:14.420
Film is a very specific world in which I don’t
think you really need to go to film school.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:21.780
That being said, some film schools like USC
which is so great at developing the community

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:28.620
and really the relationships that end up being
the thing that truly becomes the reason you

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.110
spent all that money.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:36.750
But all of the gear and the history of film
and the lessons, you can learn about how to

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.319
direct a scene or whatever.

00:02:38.319 --> 00:02:44.049
All of that stuff is in books and on the Internet
and on directors and actors giving talks.

00:02:44.049 --> 00:02:50.340
And it’s just my opinion that I don’t
think film school is necessary for filmmakers.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.670
But I don’t want to discourage someone who
really wants to learn that way.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.040
Film Courage: Sure.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.069
Did you realize that the film BRIGSBY BEAR
was going to be such an inspiration for fan-obsessed

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:01.069
filmmakers?

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.069
Dave McCary: No…

00:03:02.069 --> 00:03:04.330
Film Courage: Because it’s an incredible
story in itself, probably one of the best

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.330
movies I’ve seen so far.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.330
Dave McCary: Oh my Gosh.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:07.330
Thank you!

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.799
Film Courage: And it caters to people who
are just obsessed with movies.

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:19.030
Dave McCary: Well we definitely felt that
it was a love letter to storytelling and filmmaking

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.780
in the movie-going experience.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.069
But you never want to make something with
the expectation of it having that type of

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:28.069
reception.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:33.129
I think that’s just unhealthy to hope for
that or expect that.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:40.099
We were just trying to have fun and make something
that we liked and that maybe our friends liked

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:46.329
but we weren’t trying to build any type
of obsession.

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:52.569
It’s always nice to hear that people are
inspired by it but I was never thinking “I’m

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:59.500
going to make a film that everyone’s inspired
by,” which it’s definitely not everyone,

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:06.889
but it’s very nice to hear that it gives
people that energy of “Oh..I’m excited

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:09.099
to go make a video after watching this movie.”

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:10.450
That’s a cool feeling.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:14.360
Film Courage: So similar to making Youtube
videos because you never know when the audience

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.000
is going to relate to one of your comedy skits
versus not (it’s hit or miss)?

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.190
Dave McCary: Yeah.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:26.070
Again with every Internet video we made (even
our Saturday Night Live sketches), we’re

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:33.669
trying to be the best we can at not catering
to any audiences and just trying to make each

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:37.780
other laugh or each other feel or whatever
it is.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:44.639
But the repetition of all those videos that
we’ve made (the hundreds over the years)

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:47.770
was so instrumental going into making this
film.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:54.090
And this film really is…it mirrors both
Kyle and my experience as both best friends

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.810
who fell in love with filmmaking together
and the movie has a lot to do about collaborating

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:07.400
with a best friend and following them up with
telling stories so it’s a very meta experience

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.259
and it’s super special.

